1
00:00:11,380 --> 00:00:17,540
Floating on the Nakagawa,
aiming for one certain day,

2
00:00:17,540 --> 00:00:24,660
I kick a pebble and even my shoes go flying,

3
00:00:24,660 --> 00:00:32,940
onto the head of the carpenter
I was putting in a choke hold.

4
00:00:32,940 --> 00:00:39,160
They laughed at me for
staying a kid at heart.

5
00:00:39,780 --> 00:00:44,220
Even if I chase after some energy somewhere,

6
00:00:44,220 --> 00:00:49,280
passion or not,
one yawn and nothing changes.

7
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:53,160
The streets feel strangely gentle.

8
00:00:54,660 --> 00:01:01,180
From the pointed hat,
from the purifying water spout,

9
00:01:01,180 --> 00:01:07,460
the sun sets toward the vast sky.

10
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:15,920
Tomorrow will end just like this

11
00:01:15,920 --> 00:01:21,020
In Katsushika Shibamata,
even happiness gets lost.

12
00:01:21,020 --> 00:01:24,500
I'm a fool who only
realized it after hearing it.

13
00:01:39,280 --> 00:01:43,260
Okay, get closer together. This is a wonderful Japanese souvenir,
isn't it?

14
00:01:43,260 --> 00:01:47,620
Hold still and smile, smile, smile.

15
00:01:48,420 --> 00:01:50,680
Okay, say cheese!

16
00:01:54,360 --> 00:01:56,380
Senpai, where have you been?

17
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:58,920
A bit of US-Japan friendship

18
00:01:59,700 --> 00:02:03,440
But there have always been lots
of foreign tourists in Asakusa.

19
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:07,220
Now that I look closely,
it really is nothing but foreigners.

20
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:09,480
When it comes to Akihabara and Asakusa,

21
00:02:09,480 --> 00:02:12,800
they're always featured in guidebooks for foreigners,
you know.

22
00:02:14,160 --> 00:02:14,520
What?

23
00:02:17,820 --> 00:02:19,860
Japan's roads are so narrow!

24
00:02:20,280 --> 00:02:23,280
Damn it,
driving a car into a place like this!

25
00:02:23,280 --> 00:02:25,400
Stop! Halt!

26
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:36,040
Are you all right, senpai?

27
00:02:36,580 --> 00:02:37,540
Somehow, yeah.

28
00:02:37,540 --> 00:02:39,420
What a guy, though.

29
00:02:40,540 --> 00:02:41,700
It's a Jaguar.

30
00:02:41,700 --> 00:02:43,580
And it's an old Mark 2 model, too.

31
00:02:43,580 --> 00:02:44,960
An Englishman

32
00:02:45,440 --> 00:02:47,300
Suddenly jumping right in front of the car!

33
00:02:47,300 --> 00:02:49,660
It's common sense in England.

34
00:02:49,660 --> 00:02:51,440
Hey, what the hell are you thinking?

35
00:02:53,520 --> 00:02:57,680
I thought we were in Japan, but we've ended up
all the way in Africa with the mountain gorillas?

36
00:02:58,320 --> 00:02:59,640
Who're you calling a gorilla?

37
00:03:01,320 --> 00:03:04,120
Professor Ezaki! Isn't that Professor Ezaki?

38
00:03:06,560 --> 00:03:08,320
What, Nakagawa, you know him?

39
00:03:08,320 --> 00:03:12,580
Yes, when I was studying abroad
at Cambridge University in England,

40
00:03:13,440 --> 00:03:16,080
Professor, do you not remember?
It's Nakagawa.

41
00:03:17,660 --> 00:03:19,840
Nakagawa, my former student!

42
00:03:21,820 --> 00:03:23,600
I never expected to meet you here.

43
00:03:24,060 --> 00:03:25,460
I'm delighted too.

44
00:03:25,460 --> 00:03:28,880
To see you again in a foreign
land at the far end of the East!

45
00:03:28,880 --> 00:03:31,240
It truly is a miracle.

46
00:03:31,240 --> 00:03:32,600
It's a miracle.

47
00:03:33,020 --> 00:03:35,960
I can't tell if you're
Japanese or a foreigner.

48
00:03:35,960 --> 00:03:38,580
I've lived abroad for a long time,
after all.

49
00:03:39,600 --> 00:03:42,180
It's just about time for morning tea.

50
00:03:42,180 --> 00:03:43,066
Well then,

51
00:03:43,116 --> 00:03:46,300
let's sit over there
and catch up on old times.

52
00:03:48,520 --> 00:03:50,200
What's with that outfit?

53
00:03:51,440 --> 00:03:52,560
You're wearing a skirt, of all things?

54
00:03:53,380 --> 00:03:55,740
This is the traditional
dress of an English gentleman!

55
00:03:56,620 --> 00:03:58,060
It's a kilt, senpai.

56
00:03:58,060 --> 00:04:00,620
It's a traditional
national costume of Britain.

57
00:04:02,460 --> 00:04:04,420
But I'm pretty sure it's not from England,

58
00:04:04,420 --> 00:04:05,980
I think it's from Scotland.

59
00:04:07,140 --> 00:04:09,260
Hey, Nakagawa, this gentleman is...

60
00:04:10,500 --> 00:04:11,580
...wearing trousers now.

61
00:04:12,800 --> 00:04:15,460
Once again, one look at your
getup and I know right away.

62
00:04:15,820 --> 00:04:17,480
Did you change because you got it wrong?

63
00:04:18,300 --> 00:04:19,280
This is my...

64
00:04:19,280 --> 00:04:20,560
You're the criminal in transit, aren't you?

65
00:04:23,240 --> 00:04:25,240
I'm wearing a uniform too, you know.

66
00:04:25,620 --> 00:04:27,060
This is our senior, Mr. Ryotsu.

67
00:04:27,640 --> 00:04:30,860
Never mind that.
My Jaguar isn't damaged, is it?

68
00:04:30,860 --> 00:04:34,800
If your Jaguar is so precious,
don't drive down a narrow road like this!

69
00:04:36,560 --> 00:04:36,920
You there!

70
00:04:37,700 --> 00:04:38,420
W-what?

71
00:04:39,260 --> 00:04:41,520
The pronunciation's wrong. It's "Jaguar."

72
00:04:41,520 --> 00:04:43,660
J-A-G-U-A-R

73
00:04:45,020 --> 00:04:46,220
It's "Jag-u-ar."

74
00:04:46,700 --> 00:04:47,920
Okay, go on, try it.

75
00:04:48,380 --> 00:04:51,660
Ja, ja, jag, gun, gun, ak-see!

76
00:04:51,800 --> 00:04:52,520
Jaguar!

77
00:04:53,220 --> 00:04:57,480
Ridiculous! In Japan, it's "Jaguar"! Jaguar!

78
00:04:58,320 --> 00:05:00,500
Oh no, my tea time is going to be over.

79
00:05:01,220 --> 00:05:01,960
And on top of that...

80
00:05:04,840 --> 00:05:07,300
Well, you two, don't hold back.
Go ahead and help yourselves.

81
00:05:07,520 --> 00:05:11,060
Honestly, what a nuisance!
Setting up a table in a place like this!

82
00:05:11,520 --> 00:05:14,360
Without tea, one cannot speak of England.

83
00:05:16,260 --> 00:05:19,420
The man who knows the difference,
Professor Korosuke Ezaki.

84
00:05:19,420 --> 00:05:20,400
Korosuke?

85
00:05:20,580 --> 00:05:22,440
Bleh! What do you think you're doing?

86
00:05:22,760 --> 00:05:26,980
You were just thinking he's more like a croquette than a Korosuke,
weren't you?

87
00:05:26,980 --> 00:05:29,580
Only you would come up
with something like that.

88
00:05:30,160 --> 00:05:32,500
The River Thames, shrouded in mist.

89
00:05:32,500 --> 00:05:34,740
It was raining in London again today.

90
00:05:37,480 --> 00:05:40,200
Knock off the Anglophile act already.

91
00:05:40,200 --> 00:05:43,500
So you want us to call nikujaga
"nikujaguar" now?

92
00:05:44,360 --> 00:05:48,080
Hahaha, well,
I did live abroad for a long time.

93
00:05:48,700 --> 00:05:51,360
Looking back, my days in England
bring back such fond memories.

94
00:05:51,960 --> 00:05:54,780
The chimes of Big Ben
ringing through the mist,

95
00:05:55,360 --> 00:05:56,880
That's the bell of Sensō-ji.

96
00:05:59,540 --> 00:06:03,120
A double-decker bus rolls
past the apartment window.

97
00:06:03,120 --> 00:06:04,000
Sorry to keep you waiting.

98
00:06:04,460 --> 00:06:07,880
Ladies and gentlemen,
we have arrived at Asahi-san Sensō-ji.

99
00:06:08,380 --> 00:06:11,860
Double-decker buses are running in Japan these days,
you know.

100
00:06:13,080 --> 00:06:16,340
Baker Street has the famous
detective Sherlock Holmes.

101
00:06:16,340 --> 00:06:20,080
Myōjin-shita in Kanda has Zenigata Heiji.

102
00:06:20,080 --> 00:06:23,860
London, of course,
has the famous Tower Bridge.

103
00:06:23,860 --> 00:06:27,920
Japan has the famous Casino Star too,
even if it's not open right now.

104
00:06:29,300 --> 00:06:31,240
What a sore loser you are.

105
00:06:31,240 --> 00:06:32,000
Same to you.

106
00:06:32,460 --> 00:06:39,360
Isn't it brunch time?

107
00:06:39,940 --> 00:06:41,340
What's brunch?

108
00:06:41,940 --> 00:06:44,140
It's a meal that combines
breakfast and lunch.

109
00:06:44,660 --> 00:06:45,660
How about it, Professor?

110
00:06:45,660 --> 00:06:48,180
Would you like to get some sushi?

111
00:06:48,900 --> 00:06:50,620
That sounds good, Nakagawa-kun.

112
00:06:51,340 --> 00:06:52,200
Burrick.

113
00:06:52,200 --> 00:06:54,160
Let's go here.

114
00:06:57,180 --> 00:06:58,300
Something's odd.

115
00:06:58,300 --> 00:07:00,060
What's wrong, Professor?

116
00:07:01,420 --> 00:07:02,880
Where's the Nab?

117
00:07:03,660 --> 00:07:05,880
Didn't they already
decide on the repossession?

118
00:07:05,880 --> 00:07:07,200
Welcome!

119
00:07:07,200 --> 00:07:09,400
It's Ryo-chan and Kei-chan.

120
00:07:09,940 --> 00:07:12,060
What, Reiko? You came too?

121
00:07:12,940 --> 00:07:14,940
You two are still on duty, aren't you?

122
00:07:15,320 --> 00:07:16,980
Lunch break is fine, don't worry about it.

123
00:07:17,900 --> 00:07:21,720
Old man, I'll have toro, salmon roe,
sea urchin, and then a natto roll.

124
00:07:21,720 --> 00:07:25,080
Kei-chan, who's this gentleman?

125
00:07:25,860 --> 00:07:26,820
Let me introduce him.

126
00:07:27,220 --> 00:07:31,360
He was my mentor at Cambridge University,
Professor Korosuke Ezaki.

127
00:07:32,080 --> 00:07:36,400
This is Officer Reiko Akimoto
of the Kōen-mae police box.

128
00:07:36,400 --> 00:07:39,620
Good day, Mademoiselle Reiko.

129
00:07:41,360 --> 00:07:44,320
Isn't "mademoiselle" French?

130
00:07:45,740 --> 00:07:49,500
Oh no, Mad-, mo-, I mean,

131
00:07:50,080 --> 00:07:52,940
I mean, please open the window more.

132
00:07:53,500 --> 00:07:55,340
That's way too pushy, honestly.

133
00:07:57,220 --> 00:07:59,721
Since the professor has
lived abroad for so long,

134
00:07:59,771 --> 00:08:02,040
he never lacks wit in his conversation.

135
00:08:02,040 --> 00:08:02,740
Wit?

136
00:08:02,740 --> 00:08:02,960
What?

137
00:08:03,400 --> 00:08:05,820
It means things like "kichi" or "kiten."

138
00:08:06,300 --> 00:08:08,620
Anyway, Professor,
what would you like to eat?

139
00:08:09,440 --> 00:08:10,160
How much?

140
00:08:11,780 --> 00:08:13,660
Master, I'll have some how much.

141
00:08:13,660 --> 00:08:15,320
You mean hamachi?

142
00:08:15,320 --> 00:08:17,040
No, no, how much.

143
00:08:17,040 --> 00:08:18,920
Well, you can't expect me to know that.

144
00:08:18,920 --> 00:08:20,360
Got it.

145
00:08:20,360 --> 00:08:22,340
How much? How much is it?

146
00:08:22,540 --> 00:08:24,300
In other words, it means "how much."

147
00:08:24,940 --> 00:08:25,740
Yes.

148
00:08:26,220 --> 00:08:29,020
This Raji, very good, very understanding.

149
00:08:29,480 --> 00:08:31,000
So "ikura" is "how much"?

150
00:08:32,740 --> 00:08:33,620
Is it like a punchline?

151
00:08:33,800 --> 00:08:35,480
Here you go, ikura!

152
00:08:43,940 --> 00:08:45,720
Getting it done by today is unreasonable.

153
00:08:46,120 --> 00:08:47,000
No way, even so.

154
00:08:47,980 --> 00:08:50,080
No matter what.

155
00:08:51,340 --> 00:08:52,960
Come on, really.

156
00:08:52,960 --> 00:08:55,860
Very nice with the joke

157
00:08:58,260 --> 00:09:00,120
Was it really that funny?

158
00:09:00,600 --> 00:09:01,680
What nonsense.

159
00:09:02,740 --> 00:09:05,960
Grabbing it with your hands and tossing
it in your mouth like this is the best way.

160
00:09:08,660 --> 00:09:09,740
That was delicious.

161
00:09:09,740 --> 00:09:11,580
Pops, give me some agari.

162
00:09:11,580 --> 00:09:13,240
I'll have some too.

163
00:09:13,760 --> 00:09:14,900
I'd like some too, please.

164
00:09:15,620 --> 00:09:16,780
Would you like some, Professor?

165
00:09:18,500 --> 00:09:20,820
Then, Darjeeling tea with two sugars.

166
00:09:21,520 --> 00:09:22,660
Tea again?

167
00:09:22,660 --> 00:09:25,780
English gentlemen always drink tea.

168
00:09:25,780 --> 00:09:29,560
Sorry, customer,
but this is an Edomae sushi shop.

169
00:09:29,560 --> 00:09:31,460
We don't serve tea like that here.

170
00:09:32,740 --> 00:09:33,740
What was it? Why?

171
00:09:33,960 --> 00:09:37,820
The shop owner seemed angry, didn't he?

172
00:09:37,980 --> 00:09:38,820
Yes, he did.

173
00:09:39,280 --> 00:09:40,920
Miss.

174
00:09:41,620 --> 00:09:44,280
Allow me to drive you home in my Jaguar.

175
00:09:44,480 --> 00:09:46,940
Hey! He's linked arms
with her before we knew it!

176
00:09:47,160 --> 00:09:50,320
But I came by car too.

177
00:09:50,540 --> 00:09:53,553
Leave that tiny Japanese car behind.

178
00:09:53,603 --> 00:09:55,700
It's a Jaguar, Jaguar.

179
00:09:55,940 --> 00:09:58,180
But Reiko's car too...

180
00:09:58,180 --> 00:10:00,700
It's a Porsche.

181
00:10:02,520 --> 00:10:04,700
Ferraris are pretty good too, you know.

182
00:10:05,160 --> 00:10:08,560
A car can't just look cool and be fast.

183
00:10:09,080 --> 00:10:13,180
Without the refinement of a British car,
you can't call it a gentleman's ride.

184
00:10:14,160 --> 00:10:15,140
That's certainly true.

185
00:10:15,920 --> 00:10:17,540
Even among the British cars I own,

186
00:10:17,900 --> 00:10:20,676
Morgan, Lotus, Aston Martin,

187
00:10:20,726 --> 00:10:22,660
Rolls-Royce, and

188
00:10:22,660 --> 00:10:26,220
Anyway, England is the true birthplace
of the Industrial Revolution.

189
00:10:26,220 --> 00:10:29,240
It's a grown-up nation of tradition and dignity,
my friends.

190
00:10:30,800 --> 00:10:34,080
Hey, you bastard, you've been listening quietly this
whole time and now you're getting full of yourself!

191
00:10:34,080 --> 00:10:37,020
Saying "Igirisu, Igirisu" over and over,
like some grasshopper, kirigirisu!

192
00:10:37,020 --> 00:10:40,320
Senpai, you haven't shut up for a second!

193
00:10:40,320 --> 00:10:44,220
Japan has its own tradition and dignity,
too, you know.

194
00:10:44,220 --> 00:10:46,640
For one thing, you're Japanese, you idiot!

195
00:10:46,640 --> 00:10:49,260
Well, I've lived abroad for a long time,
you see.

196
00:10:51,040 --> 00:10:53,540
I miss London's gray skies.

197
00:10:54,180 --> 00:10:56,000
Tokyo's skies are too blue.

198
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:58,620
Where's the blue? Look for yourself!

199
00:10:58,620 --> 00:11:00,620
The smog's gray enough already, isn't it!

200
00:11:00,800 --> 00:11:04,240
There are loads of other gray
eminences and gray politicians, too!

201
00:11:04,480 --> 00:11:07,040
You two are so competitive.
Ryo-chan, you're not backing down either.

202
00:11:07,560 --> 00:11:08,960
Well then, I'll leave the rest to you.

203
00:11:13,720 --> 00:11:16,700
Greenwich Observatory is the world's standard time,
after all,

204
00:11:16,780 --> 00:11:19,380
and all Englishmen speak English, you know.

205
00:11:19,500 --> 00:11:22,240
Tsukudani and instant ramen
are Japanese inventions.

206
00:11:22,860 --> 00:11:24,740
London has London boots.

207
00:11:24,860 --> 00:11:28,040
England has geta, zori,
waraji, and bamboo stilts, but -

208
00:11:28,620 --> 00:11:30,940
does it have Mt. Fuji?
Does it have Tokyo Tower?

209
00:11:31,540 --> 00:11:34,040
No, of course it doesn't.

210
00:11:34,040 --> 00:11:37,820
England doesn't have Mt.
Fuji or Tokyo Tower!

211
00:11:38,120 --> 00:11:39,540
That much is a fact!

212
00:11:39,880 --> 00:11:42,060
See? Japan wins!

213
00:11:42,550 --> 00:11:44,920
But Japan doesn't have 007.

214
00:11:44,920 --> 00:11:48,800
If it's spies you want, there's the ninja,
with an even longer history.

215
00:11:49,460 --> 00:11:50,780
Ninja

216
00:11:50,780 --> 00:11:55,160
Got it? Tradition and prestige
aren't England's exclusive patent.

217
00:11:55,160 --> 00:11:58,520
Eeeeeh, if it comes to this -

218
00:11:59,900 --> 00:12:00,840
Pedigree!

219
00:12:00,840 --> 00:12:08,740
Let's settle it by pedigree.

220
00:12:08,740 --> 00:12:11,040
No way.

221
00:12:21,580 --> 00:12:24,200
Really, pedigree.

222
00:12:43,920 --> 00:12:45,580
Isn't that Tokyo Tower?

223
00:12:46,100 --> 00:12:49,120
What does Tokyo Tower
have to do with pedigree?

224
00:12:49,940 --> 00:12:51,000
A steel tower.

225
00:12:51,780 --> 00:12:54,000
What was that?

226
00:12:55,960 --> 00:12:57,280
I said, a steel tower.

227
00:12:58,240 --> 00:12:59,040
Whoa!

228
00:12:59,040 --> 00:13:01,060
Mountain climbing is the gentleman's sport.

229
00:13:01,060 --> 00:13:04,660
Let's say whoever reaches the
top of this steel tower first wins.

230
00:13:05,320 --> 00:13:07,540
You're trying to shrug it off again,
aren't you?

231
00:13:08,060 --> 00:13:11,840
Fine. When it comes to tree climbing,
nobody can beat me.

232
00:13:13,760 --> 00:13:15,620
What the senior says is true.

233
00:13:15,980 --> 00:13:20,440
After all, he's said to have conquered
the public bath chimney when he was two.

234
00:13:25,120 --> 00:13:26,060
Go!

235
00:13:26,200 --> 00:13:27,360
That's cheating!

236
00:13:35,720 --> 00:13:38,480
Tch, I'm not losing to some English bastard!

237
00:13:38,660 --> 00:13:40,300
We're Japanese men, after all!

238
00:13:44,940 --> 00:13:46,280
W-what did you say?!

239
00:13:46,280 --> 00:13:48,540
That's cheating!
Since when is that allowed?!

240
00:13:49,100 --> 00:13:49,459
What did you say -

241
00:13:49,509 --> 00:13:53,600
Why don't you make a rule that you have
to climb all the way to the top like that?

242
00:13:54,220 --> 00:13:55,660
Tozaki Mislane!

243
00:13:56,280 --> 00:13:57,500
Got it!

244
00:13:58,180 --> 00:14:01,720
Heh heh heh heh heh heh heh.

245
00:14:06,080 --> 00:14:10,060
It's not bad to see Tokyo from
up high like this once in a while.

246
00:14:10,500 --> 00:14:14,760
Wow, it's been years
since I climbed Tokyo Tower.

247
00:14:14,760 --> 00:14:20,860
For a while, I'll forget that noisy police box with Ryotsu in it.
It's like being above the clouds.

248
00:14:20,860 --> 00:14:23,720
Dear, stand over there
and I'll take your picture.

249
00:14:23,720 --> 00:14:27,060
Make sure Mt.
Fuji fits in the shot, all right?

250
00:14:32,180 --> 00:14:34,360
What's wrong, dear?

251
00:14:34,360 --> 00:14:37,780
No, I thought I just saw that Ryotsu guy.

252
00:14:38,240 --> 00:14:39,720
No way, that can't be.

253
00:14:39,720 --> 00:14:41,400
I must be imagining things.

254
00:14:47,360 --> 00:14:49,020
So the elevator only goes this far?

255
00:15:00,060 --> 00:15:00,880
Eeeeeeh!

256
00:15:02,400 --> 00:15:04,480
Wow, we're almost to the top!

257
00:15:04,480 --> 00:15:06,540
Eeeeeeh!

258
00:15:07,840 --> 00:15:08,640
Right?

259
00:15:08,640 --> 00:15:09,160
Right?

260
00:15:09,160 --> 00:15:12,780
Well, let's see.

261
00:15:14,160 --> 00:15:16,960
You never know until the very end.

262
00:15:18,320 --> 00:15:21,180
Damn, it's time!

263
00:15:21,180 --> 00:15:21,840
Hey.

264
00:15:21,840 --> 00:15:24,000
Yaaaah!

265
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:26,000
Eeeeeeh!

266
00:15:29,760 --> 00:15:32,740
Saying it like that brings back
the invincible sound of London.

267
00:15:35,060 --> 00:15:36,200
How ridiculous!

268
00:15:36,640 --> 00:15:37,640
I'm going ahead!

269
00:15:39,620 --> 00:15:41,100
Professor Ezaki has stopped.

270
00:15:42,180 --> 00:15:43,460
The senpai wins!

271
00:15:44,680 --> 00:15:46,280
Victory is mine!

272
00:15:51,260 --> 00:15:52,340
Senpai!

273
00:15:53,338 --> 00:15:57,002
steel towers often carry
high-voltage current, so

274
00:15:57,052 --> 00:15:57,980
be careful.

275
00:15:57,980 --> 00:15:59,560
You should have told me sooner!

276
00:16:00,100 --> 00:16:03,600
Now, this is the real top!

277
00:16:04,100 --> 00:16:05,080
Don't let him!

278
00:16:06,620 --> 00:16:08,120
That's your sore-loser streak!

279
00:16:08,360 --> 00:16:09,200
He's going to hand it over!

280
00:16:14,420 --> 00:16:17,720
Ow my whole body still aches.

281
00:16:18,580 --> 00:16:21,440
The senior officer and the professor
are both far too competitive.

282
00:16:22,340 --> 00:16:22,700
How about that?

283
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:27,500
I can't stand Japanese people
who go gaga over foreign countries.

284
00:16:28,060 --> 00:16:28,980
Yo!

285
00:16:28,980 --> 00:16:31,020
What is it, Chief!

286
00:16:31,380 --> 00:16:35,920
You didn't go and climb Tokyo Tower yesterday,
did you?

287
00:16:35,920 --> 00:16:40,580
Of course not!
Who would climb up somewhere that high?

288
00:16:41,160 --> 00:16:43,640
Only idiots and smoke
would do something like that.

289
00:16:43,640 --> 00:16:44,720
Idiots and...

290
00:16:44,720 --> 00:16:45,660
Smoke

291
00:16:45,660 --> 00:16:49,240
So it was an optical illusion after all.

292
00:16:49,240 --> 00:16:51,340
It's an illusion, just an illusion.

293
00:16:52,340 --> 00:17:00,720
Well, that's fine. Anyway, Nakagawa and Ryo-san,
there's a traffic safety car training session for the car our station developed. Could you two go?

294
00:17:03,180 --> 00:17:09,120
A car developed to put a stop to fatal accidents,
is that right?

295
00:17:10,640 --> 00:17:11,440
I see.

296
00:17:16,360 --> 00:17:19,320
For today's safety car training session,

297
00:17:19,320 --> 00:17:23,820
we have invited the professor from Cambridge University
who was in charge of developing the prototype.

298
00:17:25,640 --> 00:17:28,220
The professor is coming
all the way from England?

299
00:17:29,200 --> 00:17:31,220
It's Professor Ezaki, your senior.

300
00:17:32,220 --> 00:17:32,940
What?

301
00:17:32,940 --> 00:17:33,880
See?

302
00:17:35,320 --> 00:17:36,040
It's really him.

303
00:17:38,580 --> 00:17:39,460
That voice...

304
00:17:39,460 --> 00:17:40,200
He's here.

305
00:17:41,440 --> 00:17:42,300
Professor Ezaki!

306
00:17:53,320 --> 00:17:55,840
As reckless a driver as ever, aren't you?

307
00:17:56,280 --> 00:17:57,790
It's just like the model kit

308
00:17:57,840 --> 00:18:00,820
that used to come apart when it hit something,
the Surprise Car.

309
00:18:00,820 --> 00:18:04,121
Hello! Sorry to keep you waiting,

310
00:18:04,171 --> 00:18:06,000
everybody.

311
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:08,960
Professor Ezaki! You show up again!

312
00:18:09,540 --> 00:18:11,360
Have you been well, you two?

313
00:18:11,360 --> 00:18:13,080
We only saw you yesterday, didn't we?

314
00:18:13,560 --> 00:18:14,360
And look at this.

315
00:18:15,220 --> 00:18:18,380
Five people got knocked
down by your driving!

316
00:18:18,780 --> 00:18:20,180
Well, they're certainly full of energy.

317
00:18:20,180 --> 00:18:22,260
They weren't knocked
down for being energetic!

318
00:18:22,260 --> 00:18:24,420
I have a lecture to give,
so I'll be off now.

319
00:18:24,420 --> 00:18:25,060
Hey!

320
00:18:26,220 --> 00:18:28,980
Now, let's hear a few
words from Professor Ezaki.

321
00:18:29,460 --> 00:18:30,000
Please, go ahead.

322
00:18:33,440 --> 00:18:37,020
Today's automotive society
centers on ecology and safety.

323
00:18:39,500 --> 00:18:42,800
and building cars based on safety
as the foundation is important.

324
00:18:42,800 --> 00:18:44,820
Well, you're saying
something sensible for once.

325
00:18:45,320 --> 00:18:47,820
He's got a doctorate in mechanical engineering,
after all.

326
00:18:47,820 --> 00:18:49,260
So he's a machinery pro

327
00:18:49,260 --> 00:18:51,500
But he's a terrible driver.

328
00:18:51,500 --> 00:18:54,420
That's why the safety car I developed

329
00:18:54,860 --> 00:18:58,620
combines the durability of Sweden's Volvo,
said to be the sturdiest in the world,

330
00:18:58,620 --> 00:19:01,040
the stable handling of Germany's Benz,

331
00:19:01,440 --> 00:19:03,860
incorporates Japanese car electronics,

332
00:19:03,860 --> 00:19:07,320
and is perfected with Rolls-Royce
technology from England.

333
00:19:08,960 --> 00:19:10,700
It's the safest car in the world.

334
00:19:10,700 --> 00:19:12,660
In other words, it's a hodgepodge.

335
00:19:12,660 --> 00:19:15,420
So even England alone couldn't pull it off

336
00:19:16,220 --> 00:19:18,080
It's teatime.

337
00:19:18,080 --> 00:19:18,780
You there.

338
00:19:19,340 --> 00:19:19,780
Yes?

339
00:19:21,980 --> 00:19:24,560
Having time for tea break no matter what,

340
00:19:24,560 --> 00:19:27,020
that's the secret to safe driving.

341
00:19:28,500 --> 00:19:28,940
Right.

342
00:19:30,100 --> 00:19:30,540
I think.

343
00:19:32,100 --> 00:19:33,360
Excuse me.

344
00:19:33,360 --> 00:19:35,200
It's so hot, I just couldn't help it.

345
00:19:35,820 --> 00:19:37,700
Professor, the car presentation.

346
00:19:38,960 --> 00:19:39,400
Alright then.

347
00:19:40,240 --> 00:19:43,260
This is the safety car I developed.

348
00:19:43,260 --> 00:19:44,480
It's the Coyote 5.

349
00:19:44,480 --> 00:19:45,360
This is the...

350
00:19:45,360 --> 00:19:46,760
Looking at it,
it seems like it has two emotions.

351
00:19:47,360 --> 00:19:49,720
All seats, front and rear,
are equipped with airbags.

352
00:19:49,720 --> 00:19:52,200
ABS multiplex collaborative system.

353
00:19:52,700 --> 00:19:54,920
Thanks to Japan's electronics technology,

354
00:19:54,920 --> 00:19:58,400
all the data is displayed on
the windshield via liquid crystal.

355
00:19:58,800 --> 00:20:00,680
And it's also got three wires.

356
00:20:00,680 --> 00:20:02,120
Of course, opening and closing the doors,

357
00:20:02,120 --> 00:20:03,320
warming up the engine too,

358
00:20:06,600 --> 00:20:11,020
and all of our other original safety
technologies are built into this remote control.

359
00:20:13,920 --> 00:20:15,180
That car, too.

360
00:20:18,240 --> 00:20:19,900
Was the Tsuman okay?

361
00:20:19,900 --> 00:20:21,420
There's no way it's okay.

362
00:20:21,840 --> 00:20:25,760
That's strange,
the engine should only start in Neutral.

363
00:20:26,200 --> 00:20:28,180
Anyway, we'd better shut off the engine.

364
00:20:28,620 --> 00:20:29,260
Yeah, you're right.

365
00:20:37,200 --> 00:20:38,260
Stop it, quick!

366
00:20:38,260 --> 00:20:39,380
Stop!

367
00:20:42,080 --> 00:20:43,820
It's not swerving away from us!

368
00:20:45,640 --> 00:20:47,640
Why is it coming at me?

369
00:20:55,280 --> 00:20:56,200
Senpai!

370
00:20:59,640 --> 00:21:00,720
That's dangerous, you know!

371
00:21:00,720 --> 00:21:02,880
What kind of safety remote control is this?

372
00:21:04,240 --> 00:21:06,120
The impact test is over.

373
00:21:06,120 --> 00:21:07,060
Don't try to dodge the question.

374
00:21:07,860 --> 00:21:08,940
But that's strange, isn't it?

375
00:21:09,480 --> 00:21:12,700
The airbags didn't deploy
despite an impact like that.

376
00:21:13,660 --> 00:21:14,360
You're right.

377
00:21:14,360 --> 00:21:15,580
What's the reason for this?

378
00:21:15,580 --> 00:21:19,380
They're designed to deploy
at an impact of 300 km/h.

379
00:21:20,280 --> 00:21:22,320
At 300 km/h, the car would be in pieces!

380
00:21:24,760 --> 00:21:27,060
We should make it a bit more sensitive,
don't you think?

381
00:21:27,060 --> 00:21:29,040
Can it even go 300 kilometers an hour?

382
00:21:30,340 --> 00:21:32,560
Professor, the safety test is ready.

383
00:21:32,560 --> 00:21:37,460
He's a terrible driver,
so it's dangerous to be near him.

384
00:21:39,340 --> 00:21:40,520
Cooperate.

385
00:21:40,960 --> 00:21:43,340
We have to test the safety
of the passenger seat.

386
00:21:43,340 --> 00:21:45,020
I'm not a crash test dummy!

387
00:21:45,020 --> 00:21:48,460
I don't trust your driving.

388
00:21:48,460 --> 00:21:49,940
Don't worry about it.

389
00:21:51,060 --> 00:21:52,680
It's 100% safe.

390
00:21:52,680 --> 00:21:54,720
Don't you dare put that
on just for yourself!

391
00:21:55,380 --> 00:21:56,180
Here goes.

392
00:21:57,060 --> 00:21:57,480
Go!

393
00:22:00,460 --> 00:22:02,220
Trust me!

394
00:22:02,300 --> 00:22:02,860
It'll work!

395
00:22:03,880 --> 00:22:05,440
Whoa! It suddenly turned into a ball!

396
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:10,060
I can't see the road!

397
00:22:10,200 --> 00:22:10,960
Calm down!

398
00:22:14,180 --> 00:22:15,720
My glasses are gone...

399
00:22:15,720 --> 00:22:16,980
Watch the road! The road!

400
00:22:22,380 --> 00:22:23,860
Turn the wheel!

401
00:22:27,060 --> 00:22:27,540
Aaah!

402
00:22:29,340 --> 00:22:30,300
This is bad!

403
00:22:34,080 --> 00:22:35,580
See? Totally safe, right?

404
00:22:35,980 --> 00:22:37,900
Just hurry up and turn back!

405
00:22:38,080 --> 00:22:39,220
I didn't understand it at all.

406
00:22:39,220 --> 00:22:41,740
W-wait, isn't something off?

407
00:22:42,400 --> 00:22:45,060
If that jolt caused some
kind of weather anomaly...

408
00:22:46,160 --> 00:22:48,920
Aaah! I can't see anything ahead!

409
00:22:50,940 --> 00:22:52,360
Quick! The brakes! Hurry!

410
00:22:52,460 --> 00:22:54,060
They're not working at all!

411
00:22:54,060 --> 00:22:56,680
It's stuck in auto-cruise
and won't disengage.

412
00:22:57,080 --> 00:22:57,800
What?!

413
00:22:58,520 --> 00:22:59,680
Close the sunroof!

414
00:22:59,680 --> 00:23:00,360
Okay.

415
00:23:03,780 --> 00:23:04,880
It's an intersection.

416
00:23:05,560 --> 00:23:06,560
Turn left.

417
00:23:10,360 --> 00:23:12,440
Next, right or left?

418
00:23:12,440 --> 00:23:13,460
One more to the right.

419
00:23:13,460 --> 00:23:15,380
We're hitting the guardrail.

420
00:23:15,380 --> 00:23:16,600
Right, then.

421
00:23:18,260 --> 00:23:19,580
Is this right, hey?

422
00:23:20,100 --> 00:23:21,800
Is straight ahead okay? Hurry up!

423
00:23:21,800 --> 00:23:24,020
Damn! Hey! Right or left!

424
00:23:24,140 --> 00:23:26,520
Dr. Sandarm! Tell us!

425
00:23:42,700 --> 00:23:44,840
This is really no laughing matter.

426
00:23:45,060 --> 00:23:45,820
Really?

427
00:23:46,060 --> 00:23:49,820
What's safe about it?!
What you made is even more dangerous!

428
00:23:51,800 --> 00:23:53,700
Not a single person was injured.

429
00:23:53,700 --> 00:23:57,180
It's all thanks to the world's safest car.

430
00:23:59,400 --> 00:24:01,020
Have a nice day

431
00:24:04,940 --> 00:24:06,420
Attention, please.

432
00:24:06,420 --> 00:24:07,980
Members of the senior citizens' club,

433
00:24:07,980 --> 00:24:10,640
thank you for choosing
our sightseeing tour today.

434
00:24:10,640 --> 00:24:12,000
We truly appreciate it.

435
00:24:12,840 --> 00:24:15,060
And now,
the Hawaii trip prize from the raffle...

436
00:24:15,060 --> 00:24:17,160
Please enjoy yourselves
to your heart's content.

437
00:24:17,600 --> 00:24:20,440
Next time,
an absolutely safe sightseeing tour!

438
00:24:35,720 --> 00:24:44,160
With surprise, with noise, with spirit,
with resolve, swiftly, and helping out.

439
00:24:44,160 --> 00:24:55,720
Decided upon, with inspiration, bouncing along,
gathered together, in action, flying away.

440
00:24:56,600 --> 00:25:00,860
Above all, sumo power!

441
00:25:00,860 --> 00:25:06,440
Everyone gather, if you come here.

442
00:25:07,220 --> 00:25:12,660
Like a dream, the final energy.

443
00:25:13,740 --> 00:25:17,120
Gaze at tomorrow, believe in yesterday.

444
00:25:17,120 --> 00:25:20,040
Paint the universe,
and if you stand on the ground.

445
00:25:20,040 --> 00:25:23,740
The star of Kameari is coming, here we go!

446
00:25:25,960 --> 00:25:27,480
This should be fine, right?

447
00:25:27,480 --> 00:25:29,000
As if it's fine!

448
00:25:37,660 --> 00:25:40,860
Thank you for watching.

